1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,543
Welcome to chapter nine of my study series on Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

2
00:00:06,543 --> 00:00:08,838
Today token IDs become vectors.

3
00:00:08,838 --> 00:00:21,943
We'll build token embeddings and position embeddings from scratch, combine them into GPT-2's embedding layer, and then open up the real GPT-2 to see what its embeddings learned.

4
00:00:21,943 --> 00:00:33,716
I ran the notebook and some experiments of my own, and one of them shows why the book's similarity search returns odd results, and how a single space fixes it.

5
00:00:34,566 --> 00:00:36,844
We have clean data and token IDs.

6
00:00:36,844 --> 00:00:41,056
Today those IDs become vectors that also know their position.

7
00:00:41,056 --> 00:00:45,338
Next chapter, attention lets those vectors look at each other.

8
00:00:45,338 --> 00:00:50,309
Everything follows the chapter nine notebook, linked in the description.

9
00:00:51,160 --> 00:00:52,347
Here's the plan.

10
00:00:52,347 --> 00:00:54,202
Why token IDs alone fail.

11
00:00:54,202 --> 00:00:56,800
Token embeddings as a lookup table.

12
00:00:56,800 --> 00:01:03,108
Why order is invisible without positions, and how learned position embeddings fix it.

13
00:01:03,108 --> 00:01:06,893
Putting both together into GPT-2's embedding layer.

14
00:01:06,893 --> 00:01:12,088
Then exploring the real thing, some practical details, and the review.

15
00:01:12,938 --> 00:01:13,788
Some terms.

16
00:01:13,788 --> 00:01:17,730
An embedding is a learned dense vector for a token.

17
00:01:17,730 --> 00:01:21,749
The lookup table is a matrix with one row per token.

18
00:01:21,749 --> 00:01:28,010
In GPT-2 the token table is called w t e, and the position table is called w p e.

19
00:01:28,010 --> 00:01:33,807
One hot is the naive alternative: a huge vector of zeros with a single one.

20
00:01:33,807 --> 00:01:38,367
And cosine similarity measures how aligned two vectors are.

21
00:01:39,217 --> 00:01:39,784
Part one.

22
00:01:39,784 --> 00:01:43,185
Why can't we feed token IDs straight into the network?

23
00:01:44,035 --> 00:01:46,744
Token IDs are labels, not quantities.

24
00:01:46,744 --> 00:02:00,069
In GPT-2, space cat is three seven nine seven, space dog is three two nine zero, space car is one zero nine seven, and space kitten is forty two thousand one hundred and forty three.

25
00:02:00,069 --> 00:02:10,173
Numerically cat is close to dog and far from kitten, but that means nothing: the IDs come from the order in which BPE merges were learned.

26
00:02:10,173 --> 00:02:16,835
If a network multiplied raw IDs, it would treat cat as roughly one point one six times dog.

27
00:02:16,835 --> 00:02:20,936
We need a representation where distance means something.

28
00:02:21,786 --> 00:02:23,617
A book check on those IDs.

29
00:02:23,617 --> 00:02:29,462
Three seven nine seven is right for cat, but it's the version with a leading space.

30
00:02:29,462 --> 00:02:32,561
Without the space, cat is nine two four six.

31
00:02:32,561 --> 00:02:40,449
The book's dog, four two seven three, is actually the token space pet, and its kitten ID is the token space W C.

32
00:02:40,449 --> 00:02:46,647
The real IDs are three two nine zero and forty two thousand one hundred and forty three.

33
00:02:46,647 --> 00:02:52,352
In the full pipeline example, mat is two six zero three, and the notebook agrees.

34
00:02:52,352 --> 00:02:58,409
And the batch example labelled I will help you actually decodes to I like animals you.

35
00:02:58,409 --> 00:03:02,846
The point the book makes is right; the specific numbers aren't.

36
00:03:03,696 --> 00:03:09,565
The naive alternative is one hot: a vector fifty thousand long with a single one.

37
00:03:09,565 --> 00:03:14,637
It's huge, and every pair of words is exactly the same distance apart.

38
00:03:14,637 --> 00:03:21,955
A dense embedding uses seven hundred and sixty eight learned numbers, and similar words can be close.

39
00:03:21,955 --> 00:03:33,549
Here's a nice fact I verified in code: multiplying a one hot vector by the embedding matrix gives exactly the same result as picking row three seven nine seven.

40
00:03:33,549 --> 00:03:37,824
An embedding lookup is just a fast one hot matrix multiply.

41
00:03:38,673 --> 00:03:41,106
Why seven hundred and sixty eight?

42
00:03:41,106 --> 00:03:48,548
It's two to the eighth times three, which splits cleanly into twelve attention heads of sixty four each.

43
00:03:48,548 --> 00:03:58,207
GPT-2's sizes go from seven sixty eight up to sixteen hundred, and the largest GPT-3 uses twelve thousand two hundred and eighty eight.

44
00:03:58,207 --> 00:04:02,214
More dimensions means more capacity but more parameters.

45
00:04:02,214 --> 00:04:15,809
In GPT-2 small, the token table alone is thirty eight point six million of the one hundred and twenty four point four million parameters, nearly a third, which I counted from the real model.

46
00:04:16,659 --> 00:04:17,246
Part two.

47
00:04:17,246 --> 00:04:19,923
Token embeddings are just a lookup table.

48
00:04:20,773 --> 00:04:23,398
Here's the whole idea in one picture.

49
00:04:23,398 --> 00:04:33,542
The embedding matrix has one row per token, fifty thousand two hundred and fifty seven rows, and seven hundred and sixty eight numbers per row.

50
00:04:33,542 --> 00:04:39,644
Token ID three seven nine seven, space cat, simply selects row three seven nine seven.

51
00:04:39,644 --> 00:04:41,701
No arithmetic, just indexing.

52
00:04:42,551 --> 00:04:44,569
In code, it's fancy indexing.

53
00:04:44,569 --> 00:04:48,397
A random matrix of fifty thousand by seven sixty eight.

54
00:04:48,397 --> 00:04:54,033
Index it with three IDs and you get three rows, shape three by seven sixty eight.

55
00:04:54,033 --> 00:05:00,505
Index it with a batch of two sequences of four, and you get two by four by seven sixty eight.

56
00:05:00,505 --> 00:05:06,978
Remember this shape rule: batch by sequence becomes batch by sequence by embedding dimension.

57
00:05:07,827 --> 00:05:15,457
The module version wraps nn dot Embedding, which is the same table but registered as a trainable parameter.

58
00:05:15,457 --> 00:05:22,873
It's initialised from a normal distribution with standard deviation zero point zero two, GPT-2's choice.

59
00:05:22,873 --> 00:05:25,012
Forward just looks up the IDs.

60
00:05:25,012 --> 00:05:36,777
On my run, one sequence of four becomes one by four by seven sixty eight, and the values are small, around plus or minus zero point zero two, exactly as initialised.

61
00:05:37,627 --> 00:05:38,397
Part three.

62
00:05:38,397 --> 00:05:39,867
Why position matters.

63
00:05:40,717 --> 00:05:42,295
I tested this directly.

64
00:05:42,295 --> 00:05:49,979
I embedded the cat sat and sat cat the with the same table, and ran both through a real PyTorch attention layer.

65
00:05:49,979 --> 00:05:58,967
Without positions, the output for cat in the first sentence is exactly the output for cat in the second, just at a different index.

66
00:05:58,967 --> 00:06:07,200
Attention is permutation equivariant: shuffle the input, and the output shuffles the same way, and nothing else changes.

67
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:10,973
Once I added position embeddings, the outputs differed.

68
00:06:10,973 --> 00:06:18,108
So without position information, dog bites man and man bites dog would mean the same thing to the model.

69
00:06:18,958 --> 00:06:22,047
There are several ways to add position.

70
00:06:22,047 --> 00:06:29,966
Learned absolute embeddings, a trainable table with one vector per position, used by GPT-2 and BERT.

71
00:06:29,966 --> 00:06:35,510
Sinusoidal encodings, fixed waves from the original transformer paper.

72
00:06:35,510 --> 00:06:43,112
And rotary embeddings, RoPE, which rotate queries and keys by an angle that depends on position.

73
00:06:43,112 --> 00:06:56,971
The book describes GPT-2 as a modern LLM that prefers learned embeddings; GPT-2 is from twenty nineteen, and most current open models such as LLaMA, Mistral and Qwen use RoPE.

74
00:06:56,971 --> 00:06:58,872
I added that row myself.

75
00:06:58,872 --> 00:07:02,515
We'll implement learned positions, like GPT-2.

76
00:07:03,365 --> 00:07:09,094
The position module is another embedding table, indexed by position instead of token ID.

77
00:07:09,094 --> 00:07:24,459
Forward reads the sequence length, checks it against the maximum, creates positions zero to length minus one on the same device as the input, adds a batch dimension with unsqueeze, and expands it across the batch without copying memory.

78
00:07:24,459 --> 00:07:25,892
Then it looks them up.

79
00:07:25,892 --> 00:07:29,733
Note it never looks at the token values, only at the shape.

80
00:07:30,583 --> 00:07:33,306
What if a sequence is longer than the table?

81
00:07:33,306 --> 00:07:41,785
I tried looking up fifteen hundred positions in a table of one thousand and twenty four, and got index error, index out of range in self.

82
00:07:41,785 --> 00:07:46,674
That's the cryptic error that the explicit check replaces with a clear message.

83
00:07:46,674 --> 00:07:55,277
And it's a real limitation of learned positions: there's no vector for position one thousand and twenty four, because it was never trained.

84
00:07:55,277 --> 00:07:59,361
The usual fixes are truncating or sliding a window over long text.

85
00:08:00,211 --> 00:08:00,903
Part four.

86
00:08:00,903 --> 00:08:03,667
Combining token and position embeddings.

87
00:08:04,517 --> 00:08:07,736
We combine the two by simple element wise addition.

88
00:08:07,736 --> 00:08:15,816
The token vector says what, the position vector says where, and their sum carries both in the same seven sixty eight dimensions.

89
00:08:15,816 --> 00:08:21,685
Concatenating would double the width to fifteen thirty six and make every later layer bigger.

90
00:08:21,685 --> 00:08:30,459
Addition works because in a high dimensional space the network can learn to use somewhat different directions for content and for position.

91
00:08:31,309 --> 00:08:34,375
Here's the complete GPT-2 embedding layer.

92
00:08:34,375 --> 00:08:39,703
Two tables, both initialised with standard deviation zero point zero two.

93
00:08:39,703 --> 00:08:45,835
Forward builds the position indices exactly as before, looks up both, and adds them.

94
00:08:45,835 --> 00:08:50,580
Batch by sequence in, batch by sequence by seven sixty eight out.

95
00:08:50,580 --> 00:08:56,930
In the notebook, a batch of two sequences of six gives two by six by seven sixty eight.

96
00:08:57,780 --> 00:08:59,297
Now the full pipeline.

97
00:08:59,297 --> 00:09:08,604
The GPT-2 tokenizer turns the cat sat on the mat into six IDs, and our embedding layer turns them into one by six by seven sixty eight.

98
00:09:08,604 --> 00:09:23,081
The book lists three common mistakes worth remembering: passing token IDs to the position table instead of positions, creating positions on the CPU when the input is on the GPU, and forgetting the length check.

99
00:09:23,932 --> 00:09:24,772
Part five.

100
00:09:24,772 --> 00:09:27,964
Let's open up the real, trained GPT-2.

101
00:09:28,813 --> 00:09:39,230
Hugging Face exposes the two tables as w t e and w p e: fifty thousand by seven sixty eight, and one thousand and twenty four by seven sixty eight.

102
00:09:39,230 --> 00:09:40,930
I measured their spread.

103
00:09:40,930 --> 00:09:50,709
The trained token table has a standard deviation of about zero point one four four, and the position table about zero point one two three.

104
00:09:50,709 --> 00:09:56,803
Both started at zero point zero two, so training spread them out by about seven times.

105
00:09:57,653 --> 00:09:59,122
Here's the book check.

106
00:09:59,122 --> 00:10:04,464
The book shows a beautiful list for king: prince, queen, kingdom, royal, throne.

107
00:10:04,464 --> 00:10:10,940
When I ran the notebook, the nearest neighbours of king were ked, KING in capitals, kers and ker.

108
00:10:10,940 --> 00:10:11,207
Why?

109
00:10:11,207 --> 00:10:21,357
Encoding king without a leading space gives the no space token, which is mostly used as a fragment inside other words, for example at the end of making.

110
00:10:21,357 --> 00:10:23,694
Its neighbours are other fragments.

111
00:10:23,694 --> 00:10:29,569
The function also assumes the top hit is always the word itself, which isn't guaranteed.

112
00:10:29,569 --> 00:10:32,975
The book's printed lists don't come from this code.

113
00:10:33,825 --> 00:10:35,542
The fix is one character.

114
00:10:35,542 --> 00:10:39,871
Use space king, the token for king in the middle of a sentence.

115
00:10:39,871 --> 00:10:44,131
Now the neighbours are kings, King, queen, prince and monarch.

116
00:10:44,131 --> 00:10:47,017
Space computer gives computers and laptop.

117
00:10:47,017 --> 00:10:49,833
Space doctor gives doctors and physician.

118
00:10:49,833 --> 00:10:52,170
Space fast gives faster and quick.

119
00:10:52,170 --> 00:10:57,735
Even so, notice that most top hits are case and plural variants of the same word.

120
00:10:57,735 --> 00:11:02,475
GPT-2's input embeddings encode spelling and form as much as meaning.

121
00:11:03,326 --> 00:11:05,165
Same story with analogies.

122
00:11:05,165 --> 00:11:09,551
The notebook's king minus man plus woman returns ked and KING.

123
00:11:09,551 --> 00:11:16,838
With the space prefixed tokens, the top answer is queen, at zero point seven one, followed by princess.

124
00:11:16,838 --> 00:11:21,225
And Paris minus France plus Germany gives Berlin, then Munich.

125
00:11:21,225 --> 00:11:28,936
So the classic analogy really does work in GPT-2's input embeddings, as long as you look up the right tokens.

126
00:11:28,936 --> 00:11:37,709
Note that the three input words are excluded from the results, which is standard practice and flatters these tests a little.

127
00:11:38,559 --> 00:11:41,070
And what did the position table learn?

128
00:11:41,070 --> 00:11:47,876
On the left, the cosine similarity between every pair of the first two hundred and fifty six positions.

129
00:11:47,876 --> 00:11:54,880
There's a bright diagonal band: nearby positions have similar vectors, and similarity fades with distance.

130
00:11:54,880 --> 00:12:01,620
On the right, a few individual dimensions plotted across all positions: smooth, slowly varying curves.

131
00:12:01,620 --> 00:12:08,426
Nobody told GPT-2 to do this; it learned a smooth sense of order purely from predicting the next token.

132
00:12:09,276 --> 00:12:10,008
Part six.

133
00:12:10,008 --> 00:12:11,878
Some practical details.

134
00:12:12,728 --> 00:12:13,758
Initialization.

135
00:12:13,758 --> 00:12:19,595
GPT-2 and BERT use a normal distribution with standard deviation zero point zero two.

136
00:12:19,595 --> 00:12:27,354
Too large and activations blow up early in training; too small and the signal is tiny and learning starts slowly.

137
00:12:27,354 --> 00:12:32,504
The book calls zero point zero two proven across billions of training runs.

138
00:12:32,504 --> 00:12:40,675
I'd call it a well tested default rather than a law: many newer models scale their initialization with the model width.

139
00:12:41,525 --> 00:12:43,451
Devices and length checks.

140
00:12:43,451 --> 00:12:50,415
Creating positions with device equals token IDs dot device means they always follow the input.

141
00:12:50,415 --> 00:12:59,528
If the model is on the GPU and the IDs are on the CPU, you get a runtime error, so move the IDs first; that's exercise six.

142
00:12:59,528 --> 00:13:03,306
And validate the length with a clear error message.

143
00:13:04,156 --> 00:13:05,109
Weight tying.

144
00:13:05,109 --> 00:13:09,801
On the way in, a token ID selects a row of the embedding matrix.

145
00:13:09,801 --> 00:13:18,671
On the way out, the final hidden state is multiplied by the transpose of that same matrix to get a score for every token.

146
00:13:18,671 --> 00:13:33,038
Sharing one matrix for both saves thirty eight point six million parameters in GPT-2 small, and it makes intuitive sense: if a hidden state points towards cat's embedding, cat should score highly.

147
00:13:33,038 --> 00:13:37,217
GPT-2 ties them, and we'll do the same in chapter eleven.

148
00:13:38,067 --> 00:13:39,753
One important limitation.

149
00:13:39,753 --> 00:13:41,642
These embeddings are static.

150
00:13:41,642 --> 00:13:46,567
Space bank has exactly the same vector in river bank and in savings bank.

151
00:13:46,567 --> 00:13:55,944
Attention, in chapter ten, lets each token look at its neighbours and update its vector, so after attention the two banks become different.

152
00:13:56,794 --> 00:13:57,711
Part seven.

153
00:13:57,711 --> 00:13:59,461
Exercises and review.

154
00:14:00,311 --> 00:14:01,522
Exercise answers.

155
00:14:01,522 --> 00:14:07,508
Exercise one: a ten by eight matrix, three IDs, and the lookup gives three by eight.

156
00:14:07,508 --> 00:14:17,412
The notebook cell for this is a template with question marks, so it raises a syntax error until you fill it in; that's intended, not a bug.

157
00:14:17,412 --> 00:14:25,393
Exercise three: positions are looked up by index, so position zero gets the same vector whatever token is there.

158
00:14:25,393 --> 00:14:34,656
Exercise four: pad the shorter sequence to eight, giving two by eight by d, and later use an attention mask so padding is ignored.

159
00:14:34,656 --> 00:14:37,863
Exercise five: use the space prefixed tokens.

160
00:14:37,863 --> 00:14:42,779
Exercise seven: slice the IDs to the maximum length before embedding.

161
00:14:43,630 --> 00:14:44,711
Key takeaways.

162
00:14:44,711 --> 00:14:48,727
Token IDs are labels; embeddings give them geometry.

163
00:14:48,727 --> 00:14:54,751
An embedding layer is a lookup table, equivalent to a one hot matrix multiply.

164
00:14:54,751 --> 00:14:59,154
Attention can't see order, so we add position embeddings.

165
00:14:59,154 --> 00:15:05,410
GPT-2 adds token and learned position embeddings in seven sixty eight dimensions.

166
00:15:05,410 --> 00:15:12,515
Real GPT-2 embeddings do cluster related words, as long as you use the space prefixed token.

167
00:15:12,515 --> 00:15:17,458
And embeddings are static until attention makes them contextual.

168
00:15:18,308 --> 00:15:25,147
The notebook ran on my CPU; the only error is the exercise template, which is meant to be filled in.

169
00:15:25,147 --> 00:15:40,604
The book checks: several example token IDs are wrong, the printed similarity and analogy results don't come from the code shown, and the fix is a leading space, and current models mostly use RoPE rather than learned positions.

170
00:15:40,604 --> 00:15:43,887
All of these are in the errata file in the repo.

171
00:15:44,737 --> 00:15:46,063
Pause and try these.

172
00:15:46,063 --> 00:15:47,191
Why raw IDs fail.

173
00:15:47,191 --> 00:16:00,192
The shapes for a vocabulary of ten thousand, dimension five twelve, and a batch of four by twenty; the answer is a ten thousand by five twelve table and an output of four by twenty by five twelve.

174
00:16:00,192 --> 00:16:03,973
Two sentences with the same words but different meanings.

175
00:16:03,973 --> 00:16:05,499
Add versus concatenate.

176
00:16:05,499 --> 00:16:07,422
What the initialization does.

177
00:16:07,422 --> 00:16:09,877
What happens past the maximum length.

178
00:16:09,877 --> 00:16:13,260
And from our own experiment, why king returned ked.

179
00:16:14,110 --> 00:16:20,740
To practise: run the notebook, then add a leading space to the similarity queries and run it again.

180
00:16:20,740 --> 00:16:26,032
Plot the position similarity across all one thousand and twenty four positions.

181
00:16:26,032 --> 00:16:28,778
And compare cat with kitten, dog and car.

182
00:16:28,778 --> 00:16:37,619
I got cosine similarities of zero point five, zero point four five and zero point three eight, which is at least in the right order.

183
00:16:38,469 --> 00:16:44,229
Next time, chapter ten: self attention in code, where tokens finally look at each other.

